# HQL knowledge

`knowledge` is the semantic knowledge universe composed of concepts, relations,
propositions and evidence. `Knowledge` is the working name for its type. This
design model is richer than Graph and is not implemented by the bootstrap.

| Type | Conceptual role |
| --- | --- |
| `Concept` | Something identified or described in the knowledge model |
| `Relation` | A meaningful relationship with typed participants |
| `Proposition` | A claim that can be supported, disputed or constrained |
| `Evidence` | Material offered in support of or against a claim |

These roles do not settle constructors, logical syntax or a proof system.
The universe can draw on document structure, metadata, links, Relation Cards,
imports and explicit derivations. Namespace visibility and imported knowledge
merge rules remain open; `knowledge` does not imply unrestricted global access.

## Documents and meaning

`Card`, `Frontmatter`, `Section` and `Link` describe the authored document model.
An ordinary link remains lightweight structural connectivity. It does not gain
an arbitrary property bag or automatically assert a semantic relation.
`uplinks` traverse outgoing links and `downlinks` incoming links; a node can
link to itself.

Significant relationships are preferably Relation Cards: a typed specialization
of Card with its own identity, typed endpoints, fields and ordinary prose or
evidence sections. Semantic and document families can therefore overlap; they
are not disjoint storage classes. Exact subtype syntax remains open.

```hql
cards | typed Relation | graph
```

Distinct Relation Cards may express conflicting claims about the same endpoints.
Knowledge must retain those identities, conflicts, provenance and evidence.
Provenance records origin; it does not establish meaning or truth. A derivation
must retain the rule/model used. An Evidence section can satisfy a structural
requirement without proving its claim.

## Projection and presentation

```hql
knowledge
| graph
```

This is a preferred example, with an unresolved result contract. Graph is a
structural projection of Knowledge; a graph presentation is a view consumed by
the host. The earlier model uses `graph` for projection, while the newer presenter
direction uses it for a terminal view. Whether to give these separate names
remains open. Neither reading reduces Knowledge itself to Graph.

`table`, `json`, `graph`, `markdown`, `tree`, `text`, `value` and `empty` form the
current presenter vocabulary. Presenters produce renderable values, rather than
changing the underlying claims. A presentation is terminal with respect to
semantic collection processing. The host performs actual rendering.

One opaque Presentation type versus named presentation types such as HmdGraph
is unresolved. Hosts may choose default presenters by result type, but Knowledge
requires an explicit view because its projections are equally valid. Additional
views should not require changes to the semantic types.

See the [knowledge model](../../models/domain/knowledge.md),
[presentation alternatives](../../../examples/presentation/knowledge-views.hql)
and [[hql/design-status]].
